### 1 pubmed:41744130 Vitamin D Pretreatment to Prevent the Risk of Postoperative Hypocalcemic Complications After Parathyroidectomy in Primary Hyperparathyroidism: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis.
1. BACKGROUND: Parathyroidectomy is the treatment for primary hyperparathyroidism, yet postoperative hypocalcemia and hungry bone syndrome remain common.
2. Vitamin D deficiency has been suggested as a modifiable risk factor, but evidence supporting preoperative supplementation is inconsistent.
3. METHODS: Following PRISMA guidelines, we systematically searched five databases (up to January 2025) for studies evaluating vitamin D supplementation before parathyroidectomy in primary hyperparathyroidism.
4. Data were extracted in duplicate, risk of bias assessed, and random-effects meta-analyses performed.
5. RESULTS: Nine studies (three RCTs and six cohorts; n = 2750) were included.
6. Vitamin D supplementation significantly reduced postoperative hypocalcemia (RR: 0.35, 95% CI: 0.18-0.66), symptomatic hypocalcemia (RR: 0.53, 95% CI: 0.29-1.00), and length of stay (MD: -0.51 days, 95% CI: -0.55 to -0.46).
7. Trends favored supplementation for decreasing the risk of hungry bone syndrome and requiring calcium supplementation.
8. CONCLUSIONS: Preoperative vitamin D appears safe and may reduce hypocalcemia-related complications after parathyroidectomy, though current evidence is of low quality.

### 2 pubmed:36580029 Benefits of pre-operative oral Sucrosomial® iron supplementation in cardiac surgery: influence of patient's baseline hemoglobin and gender.
1. BACKGROUND: The prevalence of low pre-operative hemoglobin (Hb) among cardiac surgery patients is high.
2. As iron homeostasis is often impaired in these patients, restoration of iron availability might over-ride iron-restricted erythropoiesis.
3. This post-hoc analysis of a previously published, large, randomized clinical trial (ClincalTrials.gov NCT03560687; n=1,000) assesses which sub-cohort of patients benefits the most from pre-operative Hb optimization with oral Sucrosomial® iron.
4. MATERIALS AND METHODS: Patients without baseline Hb (n=349) or receiving >5 red blood cell units (n=57) were excluded from the study.
5. Data from the remaining 594 were reanalyzed according to treatment, baseline anemia (Hb <13 g/dL) or gender.
6. Patients (pt) received a one-month course of 60 mg/day Sucrosomial® iron (Iron group, n=309) or routine care (Control group, n=285) prior to elective cardiac surgery.
7. Main end-point variables were increase in Hb from randomization to hospital admission, transfusion requirements, and cost-effectiveness of Sucrosomial® iron administration.
8. RESULTS: At hospital admission, Hb had increased 0.7 g/dL and 0.1 g/dL, for Iron and Control groups, respectively (p<0.001), with no gender-related differences, leading to a decrease in transfusion rate (30 vs 59%, respectively; p<0.001) and transfusion index (0.5 units/patient vs 1.2 units/pt, respectively; p<0.001).
9. Sucrosomial® iron administration was well-tolerated, and yielded cost-savings of €92/pt (p<0.001), particularly in those presenting with baseline Hb <13 g/dL.
10. CONCLUSIONS: This post-hoc analysis confirms pre-operative Sucrosomial® iron administration is a safe and cost-effective strategy to increase preoperative Hb and decrease transfusion requirements in elective cardiac surgery, especially in those anemic at baseline.

### 3 pubmed:40420172 Effect of probiotics on prognosis in patients with hepatectomy: a systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trial.
1. OBJECTIVE: To investigate the effect of perioperative probiotics on prognosis in patients with hepatectomy.
2. METHOD: By conducting a computer-based search of electronic databases to collect randomized controlled trials (RCTs) on the use of probiotics in the perioperative period for patients undergoing liver resection.
3. Two researchers independently screened the literature, extracted data, assessed bias risk, and performed a meta-analysis using RevMan 5.4 software.
4. RESULT: A total of 988 patients were enrolled across 14 studies.
5. The results of the meta-analysis revealed that the probiotics group had lower rates of postoperative infectious complications (OR = 0.49; 95%CI 0.49 to 0.60; P < 0.01), serum endotoxin levels (SMD= -0.69; 95%CI -1.27 to -0.11; P = 0.02), white blood cell counts (SMD= -0.37; 95%CI -0.67 to -0.061.47; P = 0.02), hospital stays (SMD= -0.85; 95%CI -1.53 to -0.18; P = 0.01), and first postoperative exhaust times (SMD= -0.85; 95%CI -1.53 to -0.18; P = 0.01) compared to the control group.
6. No significant differences in liver function indices (alanine aminotransferase (ALT), aspartate aminotransferase (AST), total bilirubin (TBiL) and international normalized ratio (INR)) or postoperative inflammatory markers (C-reactive protein (CRP), procalcitonin, interleukin-6 (IL-6)) were found between the two groups (all P > 0.05).
7. CONCLUSIONS: Probiotics used perioperatively can lower postoperative infection risk and shorten hospital stays for hepatectomy patients, but they do not appear to aid in liver function restoration or inflammation reduction.
8. CLINICAL TRIAL NUMBER: Not applicable.

### 4 pubmed:40572756 Value of Probiotics on Outcome in Patients Following Liver Surgery: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis.
1. Background and Objectives: The gut-liver axis plays a crucial role in the development of post-surgical infections.
2. Surgery-induced dysbiosis can lead to increased bacterial translocation, impairing the liver's detoxification capacity and negatively affecting surgical outcomes.
3. Following liver surgery, approximately a third of the patients develop bacterial infections, with a high risk of bacteremia or even sepsis-related liver failure and death.
4. The potential advantages of administering pro- or synbiotics before/after surgery remain a topic of discussion.
5. Therefore, a systematic review of randomized clinical trials comparing patients with and without supplementation and their outcomes and effects after liver resection (LR) or liver transplantation (LT) was conducted.
6. Materials and Methods: A computer-based search of electronic databases was conducted to gather randomized controlled trials (RCTs) that focused on probiotic/synbiotic use during the perioperative period for liver surgery patients.
7. Two researchers independently screened the studies, extracted the data, evaluated the risk of bias, and performed a meta-analysis using RevMan Web.
8. Results: Our research revealed 19 relevant randomized controlled studies that included a total of 1698 patients on the perioperative use of pro-/symbiotic administration in liver surgery.
9. Eight studies were performed on liver transplantation (LT), and 11 studies were performed for liver resection (LR).
10. The results of the meta-analysis demonstrated that the probiotic group exhibited lower rates of postoperative infectious complications (OR = 0.34; 95%CI 0.25 to 0.45; p < 0.0001), hospital stay duration (SMD = -0.13; 95%CI -0.25 to -0.00; p = 0.05), lower serum endotoxin levels (SMD = -0.39%CI -0.59 to -19; p < 0.0001), and white blood cell counts (SMD = -SMD = -0.35; 95%CI -0.56 to -0.13; p = 0.002) compared to the control group.
11. Further, with regard to liver function, we observed significant postoperative differences in alanine aminotransferase (ALT)-levels (SMD = -0.46; 95%CI -0.63 to -0.29; p < 0.0001), aspartate aminotransferase (AST) levels (SMD = -0.53; 95%CI -0.71 to -0.34; p < 0.0001), bilirubin levels (SMD = -0.35; 95%CI -0.50 to -0.19; p < 0.0001), and international ratio (INR) levels (SMD = -0.1; 95%CI -0.12 to -0.08; p ≤ 0.0001), favoring the symbiotic group compared to the control group.
12. Conclusions: The use of pro-/synbiotics during the perioperative period reduces the risk of postoperative infections, support postoperative liver function, and recovery and shortens hospital stays for liver surgery patients.
13. However, they do not appear to particularly aid in inflammation reduction.

### 5 pubmed:41712216 Routine vs Selective Calcium Supplementation After Total Thyroidectomy: A Randomized Clinical Trial.
1. IMPORTANCE: Postoperative hypocalcemia is the most frequent complication after total thyroidectomy.
2. Prophylactic supplementation with calcium and calcitriol (C+C) is widely used, but selective administration based on early postoperative parathyroid hormone (PTH) levels may reduce overtreatment.
3. OBJECTIVE: To compare the incidence of symptomatic and biochemical hypocalcemia with routine prophylactic C+C and selective supplementation guided by postoperative PTH levels.
4. DESIGN, SETTINGS, AND PARTICIPANTS: This was a multicenter, pragmatic, randomized clinical trial conducted from June 1, 2022, to July 31, 2024, at 3 tertiary hospitals with adult patients undergoing total thyroidectomy for benign or malignant disease.
5. Exclusion criteria included prior parathyroid surgery and calcium metabolism disorders.
6. Data were analyzed from October 1, 2024, to March 30, 2025.
7. INTERVENTIONS: Selective supplementation when 4-hour postoperative PTH level was less than 15 pg/mL (PTH group; to convert to ng/L, multiply by 1) or routine prophylactic C+C for 15 days (C+C group).
8. MAIN OUTCOMES AND MEASURES: Symptomatic hypocalcemia at 15 days postoperatively, assessed via a symptom scale.
9. Secondary outcomes included biochemical hypocalcemia adverse events, calcium supplementation, and readmissions.
10. RESULTS: The analysis included a total of 258 participants; the PTH group comprised 117 patients (mean [SD] age, 50.3 [15.2] years; 100 women [85.5%]) and the C+C group, 141 patients (mean [SD] age, 49.8 [15.9] years; 118 women [83.7%]).
11. Bethesda III to VI nodules were present in 57 (48.7%) and 62 patients (44.0%), respectively.
12. Symptomatic hypocalcemia occurred in 24 patients overall (9.3%), 11 (7.8%) in PTH group and 13 (11.1%) in C+C group (odds ratio [OR], 0.68; 95% CI, 0.29-1.57; P = .36).
13. In the subgroup with complete biochemical data, biochemical hypocalcemia was observed in 29 of 148 participants (16 in PTH [21.6%] and 13 in C+C [17.6%]) (OR, 1.29; 95% CI, 0.57-2.93; P = .53).
14. Adverse events and complications were similar across groups.
15. The PTH group needed less calcium supplementation.
16. CONCLUSIONS AND RELEVANCE: This randomized clinical trial found that selective calcium supplementation based on postoperative PTH values was not superior to routine calcium supplementation for preventing symptomatic or biochemical hypocalcemia after total thyroidectomy.
17. Either strategy may be used, with choice depending on resource availability and clinical setting.
18. TRIAL REGISTRATION: ClinicalTrials.gov Identifier: NCT05252884.

### 6 pubmed:40633539 Perioperative administration of CBM588 in colorectal cancer radical surgery: A single-center, randomized controlled trial.
1. Clostridium butyricum MIYAIRI 588 (CBM588) has been used to treat gastrointestinal disorders and recently found to enhance efficacy of checkpoint inhibitor in solid tumors.
2. However, limited studies spotlight the role of CBM588 in perioperative management of colorectal cancer (CRC) radical surgery.
3. Here, we report a phase 4, open-label, randomized controlled trial (ChiCTR2000033883) to assess the safety and short-term efficacy of CBM588 as a perioperative, adjunctive therapy.
4. The primary efficacy indicator is postoperative intestinal function assessed by time to first flatus, recovery time of defecation, and first intake of delicate fluid and semi-fluid.
5. Secondary outcomes are incidence of postoperative complications and immune status.
6. We find that perioperative administration of CBM588 significantly benefits recovery of intestinal function, lowers risk of infectious complications, and enhances systemic immunity by increasing circulating T cells.
7. CBM588 serves as a promising probiotic with favorable tolerability in CRC patients undergoing radical surgery.

### 7 pubmed:39051906 Effect of amino acid supplementation on short-term complications after gastrointestinal tumor surgery: the AMIGITS randomized clinical trial.
1. BACKGROUND: There is a scarcity of large randomized clinical trials on the efficacy and safety of high-dose amino acid supplementation (AAS) in patients with gastrointestinal tumors undergoing surgical treatment.
2. METHODS: This pragmatic, randomized, controlled, single-center, open-label, parallel-group AMIGITS trial was performed in a tertiary care teaching hospital.
3. Patients with gastrointestinal tumors were randomly assigned to receive either AAS or standard care (SC).
4. Amino acid targets were 2.0 g/kg per day in the AAS group and 1.2 g/kg per day in the SC group.
5. The AAS group received additional amino acids intravenously, while the SC group received an iso-energetic 5% glucose intravenously.
6. RESULTS: Overall, 407 patients (AAS group, 204; SC group, 203) were included in this study.
7. During the intervention, the actual mean daily energy intake did not differ significantly between the AAS and SC groups (25.53 vs. 25.16 kcal/kg per day, P =0.493).
8. However, the actual mean daily amino acid intake was significantly higher in the AAS group than that in the SC group (1.81 vs. 0.94 g/kg per day, P <0.001).
9. The infection incidence during hospitalization and within 30 days of surgery was significantly lower in the AAS group than that in the SC group ( P =0.031 and P= 0.024, respectively).
10. The 30-day postoperative incidence of amino acid treatment-related adverse events and other complications did not significantly differ between the two groups.
11. The postoperative hospital stay was significantly different between the two groups ( P< 0.001).
12. CONCLUSIONS: AAS was associated with a reduced infection incidence within 30 days of major surgery in patients with gastrointestinal tumors and can be a promising strategy.

### 8 pubmed:39519287 Comparison of n-3 PUFA-Enriched vs. Olive-Oil-Based Lipid Emulsion on Oxidative Stress and Inflammatory Response in Critically Ill Post-Surgery Adults: Secondary Analysis of a Randomized Controlled Trial.
1. Malnutrition in critically ill patients represents a major concern as it can lead to adverse outcomes including increased morbidity and mortality.
2. These patients exhibit an impaired immune response accompanied by increased oxidative stress.
3. Nutritional support, including parenteral nutrition (PN), is critical in these patients.
4. Intravenous lipid emulsions (ILEs), a key component of PN, provide energy and intervene in the modulation of inflammation.
5. This was a secondary study of a randomized clinical trial at the Reina Sofia University Hospital (Murcia, Spain) for critically ill patients following major abdominal surgery that were administered PN supplemented with olive-oil-based ILE (OO-ILE, n = 29) or a mixed-lipid ILE (soybean oil, medium chain triglycerides, OO and fish oil, SMOF-ILE, n = 25).
6. The effects on clinical outcomes, metabolic markers, oxidative stress, and inflammation were evaluated.
7. No significant differences were observed between groups in the clinical parameters and outcomes, oxidative stress, or inflammatory markers.
8. The within-group evaluation demonstrated an increase in total antioxidant capacity in both groups, while OO-ILE increased the levels of 15-F2t-isoprostane.
9. In addition, the results showed that both mixtures reduced the release of IL-1β and IL-6.
10. These findings suggest that both treatments had similar effects on oxidative stress and inflammatory response in this type of patient.

### 9 pubmed:39248478 Changes in the pharyngeal and nasal microbiota in pediatric patients with adenotonsillar hypertrophy.
1. UNLABELLED: The present study aimed to investigate the pharyngeal and nasal microbiota composition in children with adenotonsillar hypertrophy (AH) and assess longitudinal alterations in both microbiota after a probiotic oral spray treatment.
2. A cohort of 57 AH patients were enrolled and randomly assigned to the probiotic and placebo groups for a 5-month treatment course.
3. Pharyngeal and nasal swabs were collected before and after treatment and analyzed by 16S rRNA-based metataxonomics and axenic cultures for pathobiont identification.
4. 16S rRNA sequences from pharyngeal and nasal swabs of 65 healthy children (HC) were used as microbiota reference profiles.
5. We found that the pharyngeal and nasal microbiota of AH children were similar.
6. When compared to HC, we observed an increase of the genera Rothia, Granulicatella, Streptococcus, Neisseria, and Haemophilus, as well as a reduction of Corynebacterium, Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter, and Moraxella in both microbiota of AH patients.
7. After probiotic treatment, we confirmed the absence of adverse effects and a reduction of upper respiratory tract infections (URTI).
8. Moreover, the composition of pharyngeal microbiota was positively influenced by the reduction of potential pathobionts, like Haemophilus spp., with an increase of beneficial microbial metabolic pathways.
9. Finally, the probiotic reduced the abundance of the pathobionts Streptococcus mitis and Gemella haemolysans in relation to AH severity.
10. In conclusion, our results highlight the alterations of the pharyngeal and nasal microbiota associated with AH.
11. Moreover, probiotic administration conferred protection against URTI and reduced the presence of potential pathobionts in patients with AH.
12. IMPORTANCE: Adenotonsillar hypertrophy (AH) is considered the main cause of breathing disorders during sleep in children.
13. AH patients, after significant morbidity and often multiple courses of antibiotics, often proceed to tonsillectomy and/or adenoidectomy.
14. Given the potential risks associated with these procedures, there is a growing interest in the use of nonsurgical adjuvant therapies, such as probiotics, that could potentially reduce their need for surgical intervention.
15. In this study, we investigated the pharyngeal and nasal microbiota in patients with AH compared with healthy children.
16. Furthermore, we tested the effects of probiotic spray administration on both disease symptoms and microbiota profiles, to evaluate the possible use of this microbial therapy as an adjuvant for AH patients.

### 10 pubmed:39243332 Protein Supplement Tolerability and Patient Satisfaction after Bariatric Surgery.
1. PURPOSE: Disproportional fat-free mass loss often occurs post-bariatric surgery, partly due to insufficient protein intake during the post-surgery recovery phase.
2. We compared five protein-enhancing strategies (PES) on patient tolerability, satisfaction and protein intake.
3. MATERIALS AND METHODS: Ninety-four participants, scheduled for bariatric surgery, were enrolled and allocated to either of the following: (1) whey powder, (2) hydrolysed collagen powder, (3) plant-based powder, (4) protein-rich products, (5) protein gel, or control.
4. PES groups were instructed to add 30 g of powder or 2 gels or protein products to their diet.
5. Patient satisfaction and tolerability were evaluated with questionnaires.
6. Dietary intake was assessed prior to and during PES use.
7. RESULTS: Seven patients dropped out (i.e. loss of contact, personal reasons or post-surgery complications) yielding an analytical cohort of 87 participants.
8. The majority of patients (61%) did not experience dietary complaints from PES and could use PES ≥ 5 days of the week.
9. PES non-usage was mainly related to taste dislike (58%).
10. Hydrolysed collagen scored highest on tolerability and satisfaction: 86% of the participants could use HC ≥ 5 days and 71% were satisfied with the product.
11. PES increased protein intake from 54.7 ± 21.5 g/day to 64.7 ± 23.4 g/day during the intervention (p = 0.002), which differed from the control group (+ 10.1 ± 24.5 g/day vs. - 6.3 ± 23.8 g/day for controls, p = 0.019).
12. Whey showed the highest increase, namely + 18.3 ± 16.3 g/day (p = 0.009).
13. CONCLUSION: PES were tolerated by the majority of participants, and an improved protein intake with PES use was seen.
14. However, the taste of the products could be improved to further enhance satisfaction and tolerability.

### 11 pubmed:28983778 Probiotics for the prevention of Hirschsprung-associated enterocolitis: a systematic review and meta-analysis.
1. BACKGROUND/PURPOSE: Hirschsprung-associated enterocolitis (HAEC) is a life-threatening complication of Hirschsprung's disease.
2. HAEC is reported to occur in 6-50% of patients preoperatively and in 2-35% postoperatively.
3. The exact cause of HAEC is not fully understood, but disturbances of intestinal microbiota have recently been reported in patients with HAEC.
4. In recent years, the administration of probiotics has been proposed to reduce the incidence of HAEC.
5. We conducted a systematic review and meta-analysis to determine the effect of probiotics on postoperative HAEC.
6. METHODS: A systematic literature search for relevant articles was performed in four databases using the combinations of following terms "probiotics", "microbiota", "enterocolitis", "Lactobacillus", "Bifidobacterium", "Saccharomyces", "Streptococcus", and "Hirschsprung disease/Hirschsprung's disease" for studies published between 2002 and 2017.
7. The relevant cohorts of the effect of probiotics in postoperative patients were systematically searched for clinical outcomes.
8. Odds ratio (OR) or standard mean difference (SMD) with 95% confidence intervals (CI) were calculated using standardized statistical methodology.
9. RESULTS: The search strategy identified 1274 reports.
10. Overall, five studies met defined inclusion criteria, reporting a total of 198 patients.
11. Two studies were prospective multicenter randomized control trials.
12. Lactobacillus, Bifidobacterium, Streptococcus, and Enterococcus were used as probiotics.
13. The incidence of HAEC with/without probiotics was 22.6 and 30.5%, respectively, but this was not statistically different (OR 0.72; 95% CI 0.37-1.39; P = 0.33).
14. CONCLUSION: This study shows that the administration of probiotics was not associated with a significant reduction in the risk of HAEC.
15. Additional studies are required to understand more fully the role of microbiota and complex interactions that cause HAEC.
16. With increasing knowledge of the role of microbiota in HAEC, we are likely to understand better the potential benefits of probiotics in this disease.

### 12 pubmed:41299126 Postoperative Spirulina Platensis Supplementation Optimizes Body Composition Improvement without Adverse Effects in Women Undergoing Gastric Bypass: a Pilot Study Randomized and Placebo-Controlled.
1. INTRODUCTION: Roux-en-Y gastric bypass (RYGB) is an effective treatment for obesity and metabolic disorders.
2. Co-administration of Spirulina (SP), a cyanobacterium of the genus Arthrospira platensis, accelerates weight loss and improves body composition.
3. It is believed that supplementing with 2 g of SP for 30 days is safe and improves body composition in women undergoing RYGB.
4. OBJECTIVE: To evaluate the effects of SP supplementation on body composition and blood parameters of women undergoing RYGB.
5. METHODS: Forty-nine women indicated for RYGB were divided into two groups: SP (n =25), which received 2 g of SP, and placebo (PL, n =24), which received 2 g of starch.
6. The parameters were analyzed at baseline and after 30 days of post-RYGB intervention.
7. ANCOVA and chi-square were performed in SPSS (version 25), with a significance level of 5% (p < 0.05).
8. RESULTS: Thirty patients (SP, n = 15; PL, n = 15) with a mean age of 42.82±9.35 years concluded the study.
9. Post-RYGB, the SP group exhibited significant decreases in arm circumference (p = 0.035), fat mass (p = 0.033), muscle mass loss (p <0.001), body fat (p = 0.018), subcutaneous fat (p = 0.019), and blood urea levels (p = 0.042) compared to the PL group.
10. The SP group had significantly more lean mass (p = 0.036) and skeletal muscle mass (p = 0.030), elevated BMR (p = 0.034), and a higher percentage of fat mass loss (p <0.001), along with increased levels of body protein (p = 0.028) and hydration (p = 0.037) in comparison to the PL group.
11. CONCLUSION: Supplementation with 2 g of SP for 30 days was safe, improved body composition, and decreased urea levels in women with obesity undergoing RYGB.

