Q: In children with CKD stages 3-5, a dose of vitamin D3 4000 IU daily was effective in achieving or maintaining vitamin D sufficiency.
S: 1. BACKGROUND: Correction of nutritional vitamin deficiency is recommended in children with chronic kidney disease (CKD).
2. The optimal daily dose of vitamin D to achieve or maintain vitamin D sufficiency is unknown.
3. METHODS: We conducted a phase III, double-blind, randomized trial of two doses of vitamin D3 in children ≥ 9 years of age with CKD stages 3-5 or kidney transplant recipients.
4. Patients were randomized to 1000 IU or 4000 IU of daily vitamin D3 orally.
5. We measured 25-hydroxvitamin D (25(OH)D) levels at baseline, 3 months and 6 months.
6. The primary efficacy outcome was the percentage of patients who were vitamin D replete (25(OH)D ≥ 30 ng/mL) at 6 months.
7. RESULTS: Ninety-eight patients were enrolled: 49 randomized into each group.
8. Eighty (81.6%) patients completed the study and were analyzed.
9. Baseline plasma 25(OH)D levels were ≥ 30 ng/mL in 12 (35.3%) and 12 (27.3%) patients in the 1000 IU and 4000 IU treatment groups, respectively.
10. At 6 months, plasma 25(OH)D levels were ≥ 30 ng/mL in 33.3% (95% CI: 18.0-51.8%) and 74.4% (95% CI: 58.8-86.5%) in the 1000 IU and 4000 IU treatment groups, respectively (p = 0.0008).
11. None of the patients developed vitamin D toxicity or hypercalcemia.
12. CONCLUSIONS: In children with CKD, 1000 IU of daily vitamin D3 is unlikely to achieve or maintain a plasma 25(OH)D ≥ 30 ng/mL.
13. In children with CKD stages 3-5, a dose of vitamin D3 4000 IU daily was effective in achieving or maintaining vitamin D sufficiency.
14. CLINICAL TRIAL REGISTRATION: ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT01909115.

37
Q: Eligible studies were randomized controlled trials (RCTs) comparing high-dose (4-5 mg/day) versus standard-dose (0.4 mg/day) folic acid supplementation in nonpregnant women of reproductive age, with no restrictions on language or publication date.
S: 1. OBJECTIVES: To assess whether high-dose folic acid supplementation (4-5 mg/day), initiated before conception, reduces the risk of miscarriage and improves pregnancy rates compared to standard-dose supplementation (0.4 mg/day).
2. DATA SOURCES: A comprehensive search was conducted in PubMed, Embase, Scopus, the Cochrane Library, and ClinicalTrials.gov from inception through May 16, 2025.
3. STUDY ELIGIBILITY CRITERIA: Eligible studies were randomized controlled trials (RCTs) comparing high-dose (4-5 mg/day) versus standard-dose (0.4 mg/day) folic acid supplementation in nonpregnant women of reproductive age, with no restrictions on language or publication date.
4. METHODS: The primary outcome was miscarriage; the secondary outcome was clinical pregnancy.
5. Data were pooled using random-effects meta-analysis models and reported as risk ratios (RRs) with 95% confidence intervals (CIs).
6. Risk of bias, study trustworthiness, and certainty of evidence were assessed using the RoB 2.0 tool, OGEIG criteria, and the GRADE framework, respectively.
7. RESULTS: Three RCTs (n=5144) were included.
8. Among these, 1488 women became pregnant during the study period and were included in the primary analysis of miscarriage risk.
9. In this population, high-dose folic acid supplementation was associated with a statistically significant reduction in miscarriage risk (RR 0.73; 95% CI: 0.56-0.94; I2=0%).
10. An additional intention-to-treat (ITT) analysis, which included all randomized participants regardless of pregnancy status, showed a nonsignificant reduction in miscarriage risk (RR 0.79; 95% CI: 0.61-1.04).
11. Moreover, the pregnancy rate was significantly higher in the high-dose group compared to the standard-dose group (RR 1.10; 95% CI: 1.02-1.18).
12. The certainty of evidence was rated as low to moderate.
13. CONCLUSION: High-dose folic acid supplementation (4 mg/day) may reduce the risk of miscarriage and increase pregnancy rates in women trying to conceive.
14. These findings may support consideration of revising current recommended dosage of folic acid in women trying to conceive.
15. However, large-scale RCTs are needed to confirm these results.
16. El resumen está disponible en Español al final del artículo.

